Odin 3 Qualcomm Sensor Core data

The sensor registry inputs in this directory were recovered from the stock
Odin 3 Android BSP. They select the CQ8725S/Pakala QMI8658 profile while
leaving calibration state writable and persistent under /userdata.

Stock ADSP firmware revision: LPAIDSP.HT.1.1-01147.1-PAKALA-1
SoC ID: 706

Runtime path
------------

The ADSP/QSH firmware owns the QMI8658. hexagonrpcd exposes the QSH file
services, libqmi/libssc receives SSC reports over QRTR, and ssccli provides a
raw accelerometer/gyroscope smoke test. The generated registry and learned
per-unit calibration live under /userdata/system/qcom-sensors/odin3 and are
never overwritten automatically.

The startup service prefers /dev/fastrpc-adsp when the kernel exposes it and
falls back to /dev/fastrpc-adsp-secure, which is the working node on the
current SM8750 kernel. It starts SensorsPD before RootPD and keeps both
listeners running.

Motion bridge
-------------

S29qcom-motion exposes the built-in accelerometer and gyroscope as DSU/Cemuhook
slot 0 on 127.0.0.1:26760. On Odin 3 it also publishes a sensor-only Linux
input device that SDL attaches to the built-in gamepad. The extra device has no
buttons or sticks, so the normal SDL/evmapy controller path remains unchanged.
Configgen uses /var/run/batocera-qcom-motion.ready to enable the DSU motion
source only when the bridge is healthy.

The bridge emits the DualShock/Cemuhook coordinate convention used by the
configured console emulators. It applies the real-3DS lateral accelerometer and
pitch polarity after the Odin 3's device-specific raw transform and saved
calibration; roll remains reflected by that raw transform and yaw is unchanged.
Both sensors are requested at 100 Hz and packets are emitted after
the matching gyroscope report, avoiding the 20 Hz one-sample phase lag produced
by libssc's default rate selection. The saved gyroscope zero remains the cold
start baseline; after one second of confirmed stillness the bridge refines it
in memory to follow the QMI8658's temperature-dependent bias without rewriting
userdata. Refinement stops as soon as the first DSU client connects, so an
emulator never has its neutral point changed in the middle of a session.

The SDL device uses SDL's physical gamepad coordinates (+X right, +Y toward the
top edge and +Z out through the screen), with acceleration reported in m/s^2
and angular velocity in rad/s. This keeps the native SDL path independent of
the console-oriented DSU transform and lets RPCS3 and shadPS4 consume the same
calibrated samples through their SDL controller handlers. shadPS4 assumes the
bridge's nominal 100 Hz rate when Linux cannot report sensor frequency.

The built-in config generators attach the DSU source to Azahar, Cemu, Dolphin,
Eden, Yuzu and Ryujinx. RPCS3 and shadPS4 use the native SDL sensor instead.
Buttons remain on their normal SDL/evmapy controller, and the console sensor is
assigned only to player 1.

Run `batocera-qcom-motion --check` for a DSU packet check, or
`batocera-qcom-sensors-test 5` to validate the complete sensor stack.

Calibration
-----------

Launch "Motion Sensor Calibration" from the Tools system with the Odin
unplugged and resting on a level surface. The controller-friendly guide first
zeros stationary gyroscope drift, then shows crude device diagrams for five
accelerometer poses: face up, both side edges and both end edges. Opposing
edge pairs provide the zero offset, so no awkward screen-down pose is needed.
It rejects movement and poses that are too far from the pictured orientation.
The fit is constrained to per-axis offset and gain rather than a general affine
matrix, so small hand-held pose errors cannot introduce shear between gravity
and gyroscope axes.

The final screen provides live 45 and 90 degree tilt feedback. Calibration is
stored in /userdata/system/qcom-sensors/odin3/motion-calibration.ini and loaded
by the bridge on each boot. Re-running the tool replaces the previous values.
